================================================================================
pingap  --  vendored, and the host program of this fork
================================================================================

  Author:     Tree Xie <tree.xie@outlook.com>
  Repository: https://github.com/vicanso/pingap
  Licence:    Apache-2.0
  Version:    0.13.10
  Commit:     51025efca56f342a9a1e5e40559637f32b9140bf  (tag v0.13.10, 2026-08-29)

  The pingap workspace is vendored into this repository. Vendored files keep
  their exact upstream paths so `git merge upstream/main` produces readable
  conflicts inside known files rather than add/delete pairs. Crates owned by this
  fork live under `crates/` instead. The path prefix is the ownership marker:
  unprefixed means vendored and carrying merge debt, `crates/`-prefixed means
  ours and carrying none.

  Vendored at upstream paths: the 19 `pingap-*` crates, `src/` (binary crate),
  `web/` (React admin UI), `conf/`, `benches/`, `docs/`, `examples/`, `website/`,
  `packaging/`, `scripts/`, `hooks/`, `icons/`, `asset/`, `dist/README.md`,
  and the root build and tooling files (`Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock`, `build.rs`,
  `Makefile`, `Dockerfile`, `bacon.toml`, `clippy.toml`,
  `typos.toml`, `cliff.toml`, `.rustfmt.toml`, `.cargo/`, `entrypoint.sh`,
  `install.sh`, `publish.sh`, `pingap.service`, `docker-compose.yml`,
  `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md` and `CLA.md`).

  Deliberate divergences from the upstream tree, all recorded here because a
  future merge will otherwise read them as accidental drift:

  1. `.github/workflows/publish.yml`, `test.yml`, `pages.yml`, and
     `mark-stale.yaml` are NOT vendored. `publish.yml` triggers on `v*` tags and
     pushes to crates.io and Docker Hub `vicanso/pingap`; `test.yml` (named
     "publish" internally) also carries `IMAGE_NAME: vicanso/pingap`;
     `pages.yml` deploys upstream's site. Vendoring them would mean a release
     tag in this fork attempting a publish under another project's namespace.
     `audit.yml` and `windows.yml` are vendored unchanged.

  2. `.gitignore` retains upstream's content byte-for-byte and appends a
     fork-only section below a marker comment, so the file still merges cleanly.
     `Cargo.lock` is tracked (upstream ignores it as a published library, yet
     force-tracks it anyway) because a reproducible dependency graph is a
     security property for a deployed WAF.

  3. The root `Cargo.toml` gains the five owned crates in `[workspace]` members
     and `[workspace.dependencies]`, an `exclude = ["spikes"]` entry, and a
     `[workspace.lints.clippy]` table. Upstream declares `unwrap_used = "deny"`
     separately in each of its 19 crates and in the root package, with no
     workspace table to inherit from; the new table exists so owned crates opt in
     with `[lints] workspace = true`. No vendored crate manifest was repointed at
     it, since that would be diff with no behaviour change.

  4. `src/plugin/admin.rs` and `src/plugin/mod.rs` are modified to close an admin
     authentication fail-open. Upstream's `auth_validate` returns `true` when the
     resolved credential list is empty, which means an admin listener started
     without credentials serves an unauthenticated config-write API rather than
     denying. See the fork's own history for the change and its tests.

  5. Four upstream paths are deliberately absent: `CLAUDE.md` (agent
     instructions for working in a checkout — kept locally by whoever needs
     them and gitignored, not distributed), `README_zh.md`, and the Chinese
     documentation tree `docs/zh/`. The docs site is English-only as a result:
     `build_zh` is gone from `scripts/build-website.sh` and the `zh` locale from
     `website/.vitepress/config.mts`. A future `git merge upstream/main` will
     offer all of them back as additions; declining them is the intended
     resolution, not an oversight. The root `README.md` is also rewritten rather
     than vendored, since upstream's describes a published binary and image this
     fork does not ship.

  6. The binary is `pingap-waf`, not `pingap`. The Cargo package keeps upstream's
     name `pingap`, because the fork's own WAF crate at `crates/pingap-waf`
     already owns `pingap-waf` and cargo refuses two packages with the same name
     in one workspace; an explicit `[[bin]]` renames the target instead, and
     clap's `#[command]` omits `name`, so `--help` and `--version` follow with no
     source change. `Dockerfile`, `entrypoint.sh`, `Makefile` (`release-all` and
     `bloat`), the systemd unit (renamed to `pingap-waf.service`), and the
     cargo-deb `name`, assets and `unit-name` all follow.

     `install.sh` and `docker-compose.yml` are deliberately NOT repointed. Both
     fetch upstream's image and release assets, which contain a binary called
     `pingap`; renaming the target there would install upstream's binary under
     this fork's name.

     Runtime contracts keep upstream's names as well — `PINGAP_*`, `/etc/pingap`,
     `/run/pingap.pid`, `/tmp/pingap_upgrade.sock`, the `pingap` token issuer in
     `pingap-controlplane` and the `pingap` on-disk cache namespace in
     `pingap-cache`. Those are not build artifacts: changing them would
     invalidate issued tokens and stored caches, and break every existing
     deployment, without changing what the binary is called.

  7. Repository identity points here rather than upstream. Every `homepage` and
     `repository` in the root manifest and the 19 vendored crate manifests, the
     source footers `scripts/build-website.sh` appends to generated site pages,
     `cliff.toml`'s remote URL, and the VitePress site title, hero and nav all
     name this repository. The site and the manifests present the project as
     based on pingap, not as pingap.

     Authorship is treated differently from URLs, because Apache-2.0 §4(c)
     requires attribution notices to survive in the Source form of a derivative
     work. `authors` in the root manifest and in all 19 vendored crates keeps
     `Tree Xie <tree.xie@outlook.com>`, with `keepmeside` appended to the root
     for this fork's own contributions. The five crates under `crates/` are this
     fork's original code and carry no upstream notice, so they name `keepmeside`
     alone. The deb `maintainer` is `keepmeside` — maintaining a package is not
     authoring it — while its `copyright` keeps the upstream line and adds the
     fork's. No email address is published for `keepmeside` anywhere.

     `install.sh` and `.github/workflows/upload_asset.sh` keep
     `REPO="vicanso/pingap"`, because both fetch upstream's release assets and
     this fork publishes none.

